;;; M1a S1a.6.6 — an unbound variable reaching an `:assert`.
;;;
;;; [Q-M1a.14](../../plans/m1a_rust/open_questions.md#q-m1a14--crash-parity)
;;; named this case in its first paragraph — "a `KeyError` from an unbound
;;; `:assert` var is *caught* nowhere and surfaces as a traceback" — and no
;;; corpus file reached it until the fuzzer wrote one. Two forms:
;;;
;;; The rule's `:match` is a disjunction whose arms bind **different**
;;; variables, and its `:assert` reads `?v0` and `?v1`. Matching the second
;;; arm binds `?v0` and `?v2`, so `?v1` arrives at the assert template
;;; unbound. Both engines detect it, with the identical message; ein.py
;;; raises `KeyError`, whose `str` is the *repr* of its key, so the message
;;; comes back quoted.
;;;
;;; Fixed 2026-08-20 in ein.rs, which printed the message alone where CPython
;;; prints `KeyError: "…"` — the same treatment `CompileError` and
;;; `FileNotFoundError` already had. `crash-parity`: exit code + exception
;;; class, and both are 1 / `KeyError`.
;;;
;;; It is an *invariant violation* rather than a rejected input — the matcher
;;; is supposed to guarantee no unbound var reaches a template — which is why
;;; it is loud on both sides rather than a load-time diagnostic.

(r0 o2 o1)

(rule fire-3 ()
  :match  (or (r1 ?v1 ?v3) (r0 ?v0 ?v2))
  :assert (not (r1 ?v0 ?v1)))
